{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The analogy fails to defend the preference on Popper's own terms because it conflates descriptive survival with normative rationality. Popper rejected induction precisely because past success (survival) does not logically entail future reliability; merely labeling the preference a 'bet' or 'heuristic' based on past survival reinstates the very inductive principle Popper sought to eliminate. If the only reason to prefer Theory A over Theory B is that A has survived tests while B has not, and we admit this offers no logical probability of future success, then the preference is irrational by Popper's strict deductive standards unless one smuggles in the inductive assumption that the future will resemble the past. The target asserts this 'smuggling charge fails' because evolution is descriptive, but the problem asks if the *preference* (a normative act of choice) is defensible, not if the history of science can be described evolutionarily. Describing the mechanism does not justify the agent's decision to rely on it without inductive faith.","counterexample":["A scientist choosing a bridge design based solely on the fact that similar designs haven't collapsed yet, while explicitly denying any expectation that they won't collapse tomorrow, is acting irrationally, not heuristically."],"premise":"The target claims the preference for corroborated theories is an 'evolutionary heuristic' that bypasses logical justification by treating corroboration as a fitness signal analogous to biological survival.","successor_question":"If the preference is merely a blind bet with no expectation of improved truth-likeness or future performance, how does Popper distinguish scientific rationality from arbitrary guesswork?","target":"fb5fefc0b40ea801eb207cd97a4db4ebea984de783ee380ff95a569863eb3138"}]}